{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-754",
              "name": "Missing role guard in mahasiswa violation page causes unhandled HTTP 500 for administrators",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Missing role guard in mahasiswa violation page causes unhandled HTTP 500 for administrators"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The student violation page (`/pelanggaran`) is rendered after only redirecting away sessions whose `user_type` is `dosen`. It performs no role check for any other non-student role, then dereferences student-only session fields (`angkatan`, `nim`). An authenticated administrator reaching this route triggers a PHP undefined-array-key warning that the framework's error handler converts to an uncaught exception, returning **HTTP 500 Internal Server Error** instead of a controlled `403 Forbidden`."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The student violation page (`/pelanggaran`) is rendered after only redirecting away sessions whose `user_type` is `dosen`. It performs no role check for any other non-student role, then dereferences student-only session fields (`angkatan`, `nim`). An authenticated administrator reaching this route triggers a PHP undefined-array-key warning that the framework's error handler converts to an uncaught exception, returning **HTTP 500 Internal Server Error** instead of a controlled `403 Forbidden`.\n\nAn authenticated administrator (or any authenticated session that is neither `mahasiswa` nor `dosen`) can force an unhandled server-side exception on a route they are not authorised to view. This is an availability and access-control-consistency defect: the page fails with a generic error rather than a deliberate denial, and the same class of bug was already remediated elsewhere (`/pelanggaran/dosen` returns `403` for administrators). The generic error page does not leak stack traces or internal paths, so confidentiality impact is limited; there is no state change or data exposure.\n\nApply a fail-closed role guard to the student violation page. Immediately after the existing `dosen` redirect in `views/pelanggaran/pelanggaran-page.php`, abort with a controlled `403` when the session role is not `mahasiswa`, reusing the existing `app_abort_forbidden()` helper rather than falling through to student-only field access. More broadly, replace the ad-hoc per-view role checks with a single shared guard (e.g. `app_require_role('mahasiswa')`) invoked at the top of every role-scoped view and action handler, so that a request from an unexpected role always yields a deliberate denial instead of an unhandled exception.",
                "markdown": "The student violation page (`/pelanggaran`) is rendered after only redirecting away sessions whose `user_type` is `dosen`. It performs no role check for any other non-student role, then dereferences student-only session fields (`angkatan`, `nim`). An authenticated administrator reaching this route triggers a PHP undefined-array-key warning that the framework's error handler converts to an uncaught exception, returning **HTTP 500 Internal Server Error** instead of a controlled `403 Forbidden`.\n\nAn authenticated administrator (or any authenticated session that is neither `mahasiswa` nor `dosen`) can force an unhandled server-side exception on a route they are not authorised to view. This is an availability and access-control-consistency defect: the page fails with a generic error rather than a deliberate denial, and the same class of bug was already remediated elsewhere (`/pelanggaran/dosen` returns `403` for administrators). The generic error page does not leak stack traces or internal paths, so confidentiality impact is limited; there is no state change or data exposure.\n\nApply a fail-closed role guard to the student violation page. Immediately after the existing `dosen` redirect in `views/pelanggaran/pelanggaran-page.php`, abort with a controlled `403` when the session role is not `mahasiswa`, reusing the existing `app_abort_forbidden()` helper rather than falling through to student-only field access. More broadly, replace the ad-hoc per-view role checks with a single shared guard (e.g. `app_require_role('mahasiswa')`) invoked at the top of every role-scoped view and action handler, so that a request from an unexpected role always yields a deliberate denial instead of an unhandled exception."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-754",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/754.html"
            }
          ],
          "version": "1.4.1"
        }
      },
      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-754",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Missing role guard in mahasiswa violation page causes unhandled HTTP 500 for administrators\n\nThe student violation page (`/pelanggaran`) is rendered after only redirecting away sessions whose `user_type` is `dosen`. It performs no role check for any other non-student role, then dereferences student-only session fields (`angkatan`, `nim`). An authenticated administrator reaching this route triggers a PHP undefined-array-key warning that the framework's error handler converts to an uncaught exception, returning **HTTP 500 Internal Server Error** instead of a controlled `403 Forbidden`."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "views/pelanggaran/pelanggaran-page.php"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 12,
                  "endLine": 14,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if ($_SESSION['user_type'] === 'dosen') {\n    app_redirect_page('page.pelanggaran_dosen');\n}"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Enforce student-only access with a fail-closed role guard"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/pelanggaran",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "views/pelanggaran/pelanggaran-page.php"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 12,
                        "endLine": 14
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if ($_SESSION['user_type'] === 'dosen') {\n    app_redirect_page('page.pelanggaran_dosen');\n}\n\nif (($_SESSION['user_type'] ?? '') !== 'mahasiswa') {\n    app_abort_forbidden();\n}"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Apply a fail-closed role guard to the student violation page. Immediately after the existing `dosen` redirect in `views/pelanggaran/pelanggaran-page.php`, abort with a controlled `403` when the session role is not `mahasiswa`, reusing the existing `app_abort_forbidden()` helper rather than falling through to student-only field access. More broadly, replace the ad-hoc per-view role checks with a single shared guard (e.g. `app_require_role('mahasiswa')`) invoked at the top of every role-scoped view and action handler, so that a request from an unexpected role always yields a deliberate denial instead of an unhandled exception.",
                "markdown": "Apply a fail-closed role guard to the student violation page. Immediately after the existing `dosen` redirect in `views/pelanggaran/pelanggaran-page.php`, abort with a controlled `403` when the session role is not `mahasiswa`, reusing the existing `app_abort_forbidden()` helper rather than falling through to student-only field access. More broadly, replace the ad-hoc per-view role checks with a single shared guard (e.g. `app_require_role('mahasiswa')`) invoked at the top of every role-scoped view and action handler, so that a request from an unexpected role always yields a deliberate denial instead of an unhandled exception."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "9ce68e62939966e540a1fcf3ce3443eef41e5bdb070bcce3ef11e7f6c6551bc2"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "37deef592dbc63b40a5ae2c78a4bc0b049db0d3911a412182d41d96039c8b827",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-21 23:04:30 UTC",
              "target": "http://172.17.112.1:8001",
              "endpoint": "/pelanggaran",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-754",
              "impact": "An authenticated administrator (or any authenticated session that is neither `mahasiswa` nor `dosen`) can force an unhandled server-side exception on a route they are not authorised to view. This is an availability and access-control-consistency defect: the page fails with a generic error rather than a deliberate denial, and the same class of bug was already remediated elsewhere (`/pelanggaran/dosen` returns `403` for administrators). The generic error page does not leak stack traces or internal paths, so confidentiality impact is limited; there is no state change or data exposure.",
              "technical_analysis": "`views/pelanggaran/pelanggaran-page.php` opens with a login check and a single role redirect:\n\n```php title=views/pelanggaran/pelanggaran-page.php startLineNumber=7 endLineNumber=14\nif (!isset($_SESSION['username'])) {\n    app_redirect_page('page.login');\n}\n\n\nif ($_SESSION['user_type'] === 'dosen') {\n    app_redirect_page('page.pelanggaran_dosen');\n}\n```\n\nOnly `dosen` is diverted. Control then falls through to student-only data access:\n\n```php title=views/pelanggaran/pelanggaran-page.php startLineNumber=17 endLineNumber=30\n$userData = $_SESSION['user_data'];\n\n$currentYear = date('Y');\n$currentMonth = date('n');\n$yearDiff = $currentYear - $userData['angkatan'];\n$semester = ($yearDiff * 2);\nif ($currentMonth >= 8) { // Semester ganjil dimulai sekitar Agustus\n    $semester += 1;\n}\n\n// tabel\n$pelanggaranController = new PelanggaranController();\n$nim = $userData['nim'];\n```\n\n`$_SESSION['user_data']` is the raw authentication row returned by `UserController::login()`. For an administrator it is the `ADMIN` row (`id_admin`, `NIP`, `nama_admin`, `password`) and contains neither `angkatan` nor `nim`. Accessing `$userData['angkatan']` on line 21 emits an undefined-array-key warning; `router.php` installs a `set_error_handler` that promotes warnings to `ErrorException`, and the registered exception handler renders the 500 error page. The response is a generic `500 - Sistem Bermasalah` page with no stack trace (display_errors is disabled).\n\nThe sibling lecturer page applies a fail-closed role guard and returns `403` for administrators, so the intended behaviour for an out-of-role request is a controlled denial. The student page is missing the equivalent guard.",
              "remediation_steps": "Apply a fail-closed role guard to the student violation page. Immediately after the existing `dosen` redirect in `views/pelanggaran/pelanggaran-page.php`, abort with a controlled `403` when the session role is not `mahasiswa`, reusing the existing `app_abort_forbidden()` helper rather than falling through to student-only field access. More broadly, replace the ad-hoc per-view role checks with a single shared guard (e.g. `app_require_role('mahasiswa')`) invoked at the top of every role-scoped view and action handler, so that a request from an unexpected role always yields a deliberate denial instead of an unhandled exception.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Authenticate as an administrator (`ADMIN002` / `admin456`) and retain the session cookie.\n2. Request the student violation page: `GET /pelanggaran`.\n3. Observe that the server responds with `HTTP/1.1 500 Internal Server Error` and renders the generic \"500 - Sistem Bermasalah\" error page.\n4. For contrast, request the lecturer page `GET /pelanggaran/dosen` with the same administrator session and observe a controlled `HTTP/1.1 403 Forbidden`.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
